# DIAGNOSTICS

`fsd` prints `fsd: FAILED: ` and the reason, and exits with the reason without the status after it, when it cannot start:
`no listen channel (post=)`, `no connector to a disk (connect=)`, `cannot open a session on the disk`, `the disk is read-only`, `the disk's sectors do not divide a block`, `no volume it can mount`, `no port` or `cannot be a pager`; what it prints of the four that a call's failure causes (opening the session, mounting, and the two of the pager) is followed by `: ` and the status.
It exits `cannot serve` if its loop fails.

It prints `fsd: no /adm/users it can read: adm and none only` when it starts without a users file it can parse, and `fsd: /adm/users is malformed: the users stay as they were` when one is changed so.
A commit that fails prints `fsd: the commit failed, and the volume is read-only now: ` and the reason.

Requests fail with the 9P framework's words: `permission denied` for a permission, `adm`'s files and the read-only trees; `file does not exist` for a name or label not there; `file already exists`; `file system full`; `out of memory` for a 33rd snapshot or 65th mapped file; `operation not supported` for `Tmap` without `pager`; and `fid already in use` for a change after `halt` and a `del` of an open branch; a `ctl` command whose commit fails, with the commit's reason.

# BUGS

`fsd` is single-threaded: a request that waits for the disk delays every other client.

Its reasons are the framework's, not its own: a halted volume answers `fid already in use`, and a 17th branch `file does not exist`.
A branch, once attached, stays open until `fsd` exits, so `del` refuses it, and 16 branches at most can ever be attached in one run.

At most 32 snapshots are open at once, and one is closed only when its label is deleted; a slot freed by `del` is the next snapshot's, and fids still on it read that one's file of the same qid, unchecked.
A 33rd snapshot, by label or through the dump view, which anyone can walk, fails until `fsd` restarts.

Smaller review findings, due with M6's pipelined server: reloading the users file maps live fids to whoever now has their place in the table; `halt` still reaps orphans; `rename` and `symlink` can make a real `ctl` or `status` in `adm`'s root; a create counts no open when the 512-entry open table is full, and any client can fill it; the dump view shows the first 256 dated labels in key order; two readers of `status` at once share one buffer; `ORCLOSE` is ignored.
Closing any file called `users` in the `adm` branch, not only the root's, reads the users again.

`rollback` makes the `BRANCH@before-N` label before rolling back, and leaves it if the rollback then fails; a branch's name past 223 bytes is cut short in that label.

A hostile volume image can make `fsd` free live blocks, spin through a cyclic deadlist chain (bounded by the device's size), or make the checker slow (quadratic in snapshots); only the system volume, which the system made, is mounted today.
An upsert's room for its worst case, 32 blocks, is an estimate, past which the volume's sticky error still stands.

Identity is advisory until keyd (M10).
POSIX programs see no owners: `Tgetattr` reports uid and gid 0, and its ctime is the mtime.

The design's `fsd -c`, a check of an unmounted volume, and `fsd -r`, which reams a new one, do not exist: `ctl`'s `check` checks the mounted volume, and install(8) and `host/vxfs` make volumes.
`status` has no space by branch and no cache sizes, which the design gives it.

`msync(MS_SYNC)` does not wait: mapped writes reach the volume within about 10 seconds, or at the file's next `fsync`.
